Structural Guardrails — Mastery & Future-Proofing Spec
Working title: Key Loan Protocol (KLP) — agents borrow keys, they never keep them.

Mission: Permanently eliminate the IDP-history mistake class where agents build controls, then hold them — and make guardrail change frictionless for the founder, deterministic by default, fully visible always, and structurally incapable of becoming agent-owned.

1. The mistake class — named, encoded, banned
This is the class of failure the protocol exists to make structurally impossible, not just discouraged.

#	Historical mistake	Root cause	KLP counter
1	Agent builds guardrail, keeps control	Permanent privilege	Keys are always leases, never ownership
2	Guardrail becomes agent-owned black box	No ownership model	Founder/estate owns every guardrail; agent is builder only
3	Permission creep / silent expansion	No expiry	Time-bound, purpose-bound, auto-revoked
4	Founder loses keys / can't revoke	Keys handed over	Founder holds root; agent holds nothing standing
5	Controls bypassed due to founder friction	Approval too heavy	Frictionless multi-surface grant; deterministic auto-approve for low risk
6	Inconsistent controls across surfaces	Per-surface ad hoc	One protocol across infra, gates, CI, data, agents, all surfaces
7	Non-deterministic, human-dependent approval	No policy engine	Policy engine decides; founder configures the policy
8	Untestable / unmonitored guardrails	Built as side-effect	Guardrails-as-code, tested, monitored, adversarially probed
9	"Temporary" fixes become permanent	No enforcement	Expiry is default; extension = new request
10	Founder blind to guardrail state	No visibility	Full founder visibility always — grant, use, revoke, audit
Banned by policy, enforced by the engine: standing agent access, agent-owned guardrails, non-expiring grants, unlogged guardrail changes, founder-invisible gates.

2. The Five Laws of KLP
Founder owns the root. Agents never hold standing keys to any protected surface.

All access is a lease. Time-bound, purpose-bound, scope-bound, auto-revoked.

Build, lock, return. After building or changing a guardrail, the agent locks the gate and hands back the keys. Re-entry requires a new request.

Deterministic by default, founder by exception. A policy engine decides; the founder intervenes only when the policy says so — with full visibility always.

Every guardrail is estate-owned, versioned, tested, and alive. No black boxes. No orphans. No silent drift.

3. The Key Loan Protocol — lifecycle
text
REQUEST → CLASSIFY → DECIDE → GRANT (lease) → EXECUTE (scoped)
   → BUILD/LOCK GUARDRAIL → RETURN KEYS (auto-revoke)
   → AUDIT → POST-REVIEW (compounding fix)
3.1 Stage detail
Stage	What happens	Deterministic?
Request	Agent states purpose, surface, scope, duration, risk, rollback	Yes — structured
Classify	Policy engine scores: risk × surface × agent trust × duration × scope	Yes
Decide	Auto-approve / founder-approve / deny — per founder-configured policy	Yes
Grant	Time-boxed lease issued; scope limited; expiry set; audit opened	Yes
Execute	Agent operates only within lease scope; every action logged	Yes
Build/Lock	Agent builds/changes guardrail; locks the gate; guardrail committed as code	Yes
Return keys	Lease auto-revoked on completion or expiry — whichever first	Yes
Audit	Immutable record: who, what, why, when, scope, outcome	Yes
Post-review	Compounding improvement or explicit "no gap" justification	Yes
3.2 The non-negotiable
Locking and returning keys is part of the definition of done. A change is not "delivered" until the lease is revoked and the gate is locked. The estate rejects "done" without proof of revocation.

