6. Guardrail ownership model — founder/estate owns, agent builds
6.1 Ownership rules
Every guardrail has an estate owner (founder, or a lane accountable to founder) — never an agent.

Agents are builders, not owners. They may build/modify under lease; ownership never transfers.

Every guardrail is registered in the estate inventory with: owner, charter, scope, tests, threat model, review date, heartbeat.

Access to a guardrail is itself a leased privilege — including access to the guardrail the agent just built.

6.2 The "lock and return" rule
After building or changing a guardrail:

Agent locks the gate (final state committed, tested, signed).

Agent returns keys (lease revoked automatically).

Agent cannot re-enter without a new request.

New guardrail is estate-owned from the moment of lock — agent has no standing access to it.

6.3 Acceptance
Audit shows zero standing agent access to any protected surface.

Every guardrail has a named estate owner.

Every guardrail access is a lease.

7. Future-proofing guardrails — they must be alive, tested, and adversarial
Guardrails themselves are subject to the founder doctrine: they must compound, not decay.

7.1 Guardrails-as-code
Versioned in git, reviewed, signed.

Every guardrail: owner, charter, tests, threat model, expiry review date.

No guardrail exists outside the registry.

7.2 Continuous proof
Regression suite — every guardrail proves it still blocks what it should.

Mutation testing — weaken the guardrail; tests must fail. If not, the guardrail is dead.

Adversarial red-team agents — try to bypass every guardrail continuously. A successful bypass is a compounding fix, not a shame.

Drift detection — guardrail config vs deployed state, continuously reconciled.

Heartbeat — every guardrail emits health/last-enforced signal into the 2100 view.

7.3 Periodic re-certification
Each guardrail re-reviewed on a schedule (founder-configurable).

Re-certification requires: passing tests, current threat model, owner confirmation, expiry of old assumptions.

Fail = escalate to founder, auto-tighten, open compounding fix.

7.4 Anti-pattern scanners (encoded, automated)
Standing access scanner

Agent-owned guardrail detector

Non-expiring lease detector

Unlogged change detector

Bypass-path detector

Orphan guardrail detector (no owner, no tests, no heartbeat)

Any hit = blocked by the engine + founder notified + compounding fix opened.

7.5 Acceptance
No guardrail without tests, owner, threat model, heartbeat.

Red-team bypass attempts are continuous and produce measurable improvement.

Drift between declared and deployed guardrails is zero (audited).

