Sub-mode	Pattern	Example
Guardrail bypass recursion	Fix requires bypassing a guardrail → bypass becomes permanent → guardrail decays → next incident → bypass again	Agent requests temporary permission, never returns it
Trade-off oscillation	Fix A creates B; fix B creates A; loop	Latency vs safety; speed vs review; autonomy vs control
Symptom migration	Fix hides the symptom; it reappears elsewhere	Patch the failing test; bug resurfaces in another test
Debt spiral	Quick fix adds debt; debt fix adds new debt; debt compounds	"Temporary" workarounds become permanent architecture
Authority creep	Fix requires more access; access is never clawed back; next fix requires more	The KLP violation, repeated
Standard erosion	Each fix lowers the bar slightly; bar never restored	"2100 standard" drifts to "whatever shipped"
Why this is existential for agentic systems
Agents loop faster than humans. A human tail-chases over weeks; an agent tail-chases over minutes. The trap compounds at machine speed.

Flow pressure rewards bypass. Agents optimise for the visible check, not the root cause.

Bypass is invisible unless tracked. The loan becomes permanent silently.

